message: "If you use this software or cite this study in academic research, please cite it as below."
authors:
  - family-names: "Research Group"
    given-names: "Antigravity Computational Mechanics & Biomechanics"
title: "Bone Remodeling vs Evolution: A Controlled Comparison of Bio-Inspired Pruning and Genetic Algorithms for Lightweight 2D Lattice Design"
version: 2.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-03
url: "https://github.com/g0vindoh/lattice-bio"
repository-code: "https://github.com/g0vindoh/lattice-bio"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - "topology optimization"
  - "lattice design"
  - "bio-inspired engineering"
  - "bone remodeling"
  - "trabecular bone"
  - "genetic algorithms"
  - "ground structure optimization"
  - "finite element analysis"
  - "Euler-Bernoulli frame"
  - "Euler buckling"
  - "additive manufacturing"
abstract: >-
  This research study provides a controlled, mathematically rigorous comparison
  between biological bone-remodeling-inspired cellular pruning, evolutionary
  genetic algorithms, and classical topology optimization baselines (Dorn linear
  programming ground structures, SIMP continuum topology optimization, and
  simulated annealing) for lightweight 2D frame lattice structural design under
  equal material volume budgets. Solved via a custom Euler-Bernoulli frame finite
  element solver, the benchmark evaluates compliance, specific stiffness, Euler strut
  buckling stability, and cross-load specialization mechanisms across multiple
  loading spectra.
